WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.457
This video presents EliGSiR: continual Gaussian
splatting under bounded compute.

2
00:00:05.467 --> 00:00:11.358
Our goal is to maintain a high-quality Gaussian
map while observations are arriving,

3
00:00:11.368 --> 00:00:15.655
rather than reconstructing the scene only
after acquisition has finished.

4
00:00:17.656 --> 00:00:22.804
Classical SLAM systems are designed primarily for
fast and accurate camera tracking.

5
00:00:22.814 --> 00:00:27.129
Their map representations, however, are usually
optimized for geometry rather than

6
00:00:27.139 --> 00:00:33.555
visual reconstruction. Gaussian splatting
provides a much richer representation and

7
00:00:33.565 --> 00:00:37.722
can produce photorealistic scene renderings,
but typically requires substantial

8
00:00:37.732 --> 00:00:45.270
offline optimization. We therefore ask: can we
retain the visual quality of Gaussian

9
00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:49.490
representations while keeping the map
continuously up to date with a live sensor

10
00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:49.869
stream?

11
00:00:51.942 --> 00:00:59.762
This introduces several challenges. First,
online does not necessarily mean

12
00:00:59.772 --> 00:01:04.196
real-time. If processing takes longer than
the incoming sequence, the mapper

13
00:01:04.206 --> 00:01:10.662
accumulates an increasing backlog. Second,
computation is often allocated uniformly,

14
00:01:10.672 --> 00:01:15.213
even though easy, repeated, and difficult
observations do not require the same

15
00:01:15.223 --> 00:01:21.300
amount of optimization. Third, the number of
Gaussians can grow rapidly as new parts

16
00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:27.852
of the scene are observed, increasing
rendering and optimization cost.

17
00:01:27.862 --> 00:01:32.753
Finally, final reconstruction quality alone does
not tell us when different parts of

18
00:01:32.763 --> 00:01:35.155
the map became usable during acquisition.

19
00:01:39.466 --> 00:01:42.983
EliGSiR addresses these problems through
three adaptive mapping decisions.

20
00:01:42.993 --> 00:01:47.990
First: which observations
should we optimize?

21
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:53.257
Map-guided view scheduling selects from both
recent and historical observations.

22
00:01:53.267 --> 00:01:58.092
Replaying older views allows previously
observed areas to keep improving, while

23
00:01:58.102 --> 00:02:01.619
spatial training residuals direct attention
toward regions that still need

24
00:02:01.629 --> 00:02:04.288
refinement. Second: at what
fidelity should we optimize?

25
00:02:04.298 --> 00:02:12.238
Load-adaptive supervision adjusts training
resolution according to measured

26
00:02:12.248 --> 00:02:16.194
computational cost, avoiding full-resolution
optimization when the available budget

27
00:02:16.204 --> 00:02:19.965
does not require it. Third: where
should geometry grow?

28
00:02:19.975 --> 00:02:28.700
Existing supported surfaces are refined
directly, while uncertain new geometry is

29
00:02:28.710 --> 00:02:32.322
delayed. New Gaussians are introduced once
the structure receives sufficient

30
00:02:32.332 --> 00:02:37.050
multi-view support. Together, these mechanisms
allocate computation according to the

31
00:02:37.060 --> 00:02:38.252
current state of the map.

32
00:02:41.719 --> 00:02:48.026
We first evaluate reconstruction along the
observed TUM fr3 camera trajectory.

33
00:02:48.036 --> 00:02:55.156
Here, EliGSiR and the baselines are rendered
from the same registered camera poses

34
00:02:55.166 --> 00:02:59.803
and compared directly with the
corresponding ground-truth RGB images.

35
00:02:59.813 --> 00:03:08.713
Because these viewpoints were available during
mapping, this comparison shows how

36
00:03:08.723 --> 00:03:13.100
accurately each method reconstructs the
observations it received.

37
00:03:34.281 --> 00:03:38.413
We then render the same reconstructed maps from
a new camera trajectory that was

38
00:03:38.423 --> 00:03:45.187
never observed during mapping. This provides a
stricter test of the underlying

39
00:03:45.197 --> 00:03:50.211
three-dimensional representation. Good
performance can no longer come only from

40
00:03:50.221 --> 00:03:54.457
reproducing the input views; the reconstructed
geometry and appearance must remain

41
00:03:54.467 --> 00:03:56.797
consistent from previously
unseen viewpoints.

42
00:04:20.989 --> 00:04:23.477
Finally, we show EliGSiR
operating in real time.

43
00:04:23.487 --> 00:04:30.813
On the left, the Gaussian map grows
continuously together with the camera

44
00:04:30.823 --> 00:04:35.625
trajectory. The reconstruction remains up to
date with the incoming trajectory

45
00:04:35.635 --> 00:04:43.909
during live processing. On the right, the
current camera input is shown together

46
00:04:43.919 --> 00:04:52.403
with the rendering produced from the
current map at the same viewpoint.

47
00:04:52.413 --> 00:04:54.796
This demonstrates the central
goal of EliGSiR:

48
00:04:54.806 --> 00:04:59.167
continually improving a Gaussian representation
while keeping pace with the incoming

49
00:04:59.177 --> 00:05:01.050
sensor stream under bounded compute.

